maria_g: Hey support — customer on Dedicated reported our JSON-mode output broke their ingestion: their parser errors with "Unexpected key 'meta' in item[1]" after what looks like a model variant change. They sent a sample. Anyone seen ordering/extra-key issues recently?

support-bot: Ticket created (#4321). Can you paste the original request and which model/variant?

maria_g: Model: gpt-redwood-1.3, variant: v2026-03-15. Mode: structured JSON. Customer request body: 
```json
{
  "items": [
    {"id":"a","value":1},
    {"id":"b","value":2,"meta":{"src":"legacy"}},
    {"value":3}
  ]
}
```
Their client parser expects each item to have 'id' first then 'value' and will reject objects missing required fields. The server response we returned appears to have re-ordered keys and included the extra 'meta' key in the second item.

alex_k: Mixed missing required field + unexpected extra key. Two hypotheses: 1) customer-side schema drift (they stopped sending id for some items) 2) our JSON-mode no longer canonicalizes object key ordering and preserved model output ordering + allowed extra keys. Can we pull the raw response body from the runtime logs for req_0a9f?

maria_g: Pasted server response from logs: 
```
[ { "id": "a", "value": 1 }, { "meta": {"src":"legacy"}, "value": 2, "id": "b" }, { "value": 3 } ]
```
Note: object 2 has 'meta' first, 'id' last. Object 3 missing 'id' entirely. Client fails on first parse error and halts ingestion.

li_chen: We recently toggled a flag in Dedicated configs: preserve_client_keys = true to avoid touching model output ordering for some tenants. That change can surface extra model-produced keys and preserve whatever order the LLM emitted. Previously we post-processed outputs to canonicalize keys and fill in undefined required fields when possible. This explains both symptoms: preserved 'meta' and changed ordering. Also: our strict JSON-mode validator currently enforces required fields at item-level and rejects entire array when any item missing required keys — that makes client experiences brittle.

eng-runtime: We can do a tenant-scoped rollback of preserve_client_keys. Maria, need org id + backend request id to scope the change. Also, how many requests affected?

maria_g: Org: redwood-cust-448, request_id: req_0a9f, approx 120 reqs/min impacted since 2026-03-15T09:42:00Z when variant flipped. Customer is on Dedicated. They are ok with a hotfix for now — need <4h ETA.

alex_k: Short-term fixes: (A) Rollback preserve_client_keys for redwood-cust-448. (B) Provide a sanitizer snippet customer can run in their ingestion pipeline to tolerate missing/extra fields. (C) Add an SDK/ingestion fallback we can toggle on the fly for Dedicated tenants. I'll post a quick sanitizer example.

alex_k: ```js
// sanitizer: ensure 'id' exists and strip unknown keys
items = items.map((it, i) => {
  if (!it.id) it.id = `tmp-${i}`;
  // allowlist keys
  const keep = ['id','value','timestamp'];
  Object.keys(it).forEach(k => { if (!keep.includes(k)) delete it[k]; });
  return it;
});
```

sre-oncall: Also file a metric to track JSON-mode validation failures per-tenant. If we can detect rising failure rate, we can auto-fallback to a tolerant serialization path. Filing SRE ticket now.

customer-success: Contacted customer with the sanitizer snippet and asked if they can apply temporary patch. They want us to revert config on our side first.

li_chen: Long-term: 1) Reintroduce canonicalizer for structured outputs by default with an opt-in preserve_client_keys flag (currently flipped). 2) Update validator to support partial-item tolerant mode: either allow missing optional ids if a temp id can be generated, or return detailed per-item errors instead of failing whole batch. 3) Add a compatibility test that runs model variants against a set of strict parsers.

alex_k: Plan and owners: rollback for tenant (alex_k, eng-runtime) -> ETA 1h; provide sanitizer + customer patch (maria_g) -> 30m; SDK tolerate mode RFC (li_chen) -> PR; SRE metric & alert (sre-oncall) -> ticket created. I'll update ticket #4321.

support-bot: Ticket #4321 updated: assigned to alex_k. :thumbsup: 

customer-success: Customer is relieved — applying our workaround now. We'll confirm ingestion resumes.
